# SANDCASTLE.md (EXAMPLE — copy to `SANDCASTLE.md` to activate the gates)

**The design-rules review and the skill-discipline gate are DORMANT by default.**
The sandcastle template ships without a real `SANDCASTLE.md`, so
`parseRequiredSkillsByType` finds no type sections, every issue's
`requiredSkills` is `undefined` (or `[]` for a type that explicitly lists none), and both graceful-degrade to a no-op.
Nothing is graded until you create this file.

To activate: copy this file to `SANDCASTLE.md` at your repo root, then edit the
`type:<label>` sections below so each GitHub issue type label maps to the design
principles (Skill names) it must satisfy. A principle is enforced two ways:
the implementer must invoke `Skill(<name>)` (skill-discipline gate), and the
reviewer grades the diff against `<name>`'s rubric and `PRODUCT.md` (its
"Design rules" category; there is no separate critique stage). A principle's rubric is loaded from
`.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` (repo-local) or `~/.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`
(global) — if NONE of a type's principles have a loadable rubric, the reviewer
records `N/A (no rubric loadable: …)` for that category rather than grading
against a rubric it never read.
Delete this `# SANDCASTLE.md (EXAMPLE…)` title line after copying.

**`Required:` bullets under a `type:X` section MUST be headless-runnable
skills.** A tool that needs a live running app or external credentials (e.g.
`verify`) must be attached via a `tool:` label / `Opt in via tool:` block
below, never as a `type:`-section `Required:` bullet — the parser that builds
the host skill-discipline gate's required-skill list deliberately skips
`tool:` bullets, so a tool listed correctly under `Opt in via tool:` never
reaches that gate at all. List it under `Required:` instead and the host gate
will quarantine the issue before the reviewer's unrunnable-tool carve-out
(review-prompt.md) ever gets a chance to apply.

**Install a rubric for every principle you list**, or that type's issues will be
quarantined. The `type:new-component` example below lists `impeccable` and
`layout`, so a verbatim copy quarantines your first `type:new-component` issue
until you add those `SKILL.md` files (repo-local or `~/.claude/skills/`) or swap
in principles you already have.

## Project facts the loop's prompts point at

The loop's settings live in `.sandcastle/project.json` (copy
`.sandcastle/project.example.json`). This file is where you tell the AI agents
the things a setting cannot say. Fill in what applies and delete the rest:

- **Landing check** — `landCheck` in `project.json` is the command the loop runs
  on the run branch tip before it lands its own run pull request (for example
  `["pnpm","verify"]`). Leave it null and the loop never lands the run pull
  request itself; it waits for the owner.
- **Check folder and helpers** — first tests live in `<checksRoot>/issue-<N>/`
  (`checksRoot`, default `e2e/checks`). Name the shared e2e helpers the check
  writer must import (screen-readiness wait, graded text read, sign-in helper)
  and the seeded test users it may sign in as.
- **Protected paths** — files an AI agent may never edit (`protectedPaths`): the
  Playwright config, shared e2e helpers, any seed or fixture data the checks
  grade against. Say here why each is protected.
- **Test database** — if `testDatabase` is set, name the template database, the
  `setupCommand` that applies schema to a clone, and the seed command.
- **Migrations** — if `migrations` is set, name the project's own migration
  command and numbering rule.
- **Known failures** — a doc of failure families agents have repeated, if you
  keep one (the reviewer checks diffs against it).

### type:new-component

Required:

- impeccable
- layout

Opt in via `tool:` labels for optional, non-graded tooling.

### type:cleanup

Required: (none)

`type:cleanup` declares zero principles, so it ships without grading — use it
for pure deletions / mechanical refactors.
